A||||8||Ken Wittlief||think i sanded my paint too far, black spot showing||||1||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||12:27 AM||||I decided to see if taking the peeling clear coat off a small part of my front hood would be easy, and if it would look better. (the hood looks like sunburned skin, literally peeling off).<P>  I wet sanded a small area, and the clear coat came off, and a layer of silver came off, and there is metallic siver metal flake like paint under it. The hood must have been repainted once.<P>  The little area looked so good I decided to do the left side of the hood, up to the bend/fold in the form of it.<P>  Well I sanded off a little too much in one spot and got all the way down to the black (plastic?)<P>  I think I remember someone else saying once that is a BAD thing, that normal paint will not stick if you sand down to the black plastic?<P>   Have I done something terrible? If I wet sand the rest of the hood it will look fairly decent until I get the car painted, but now I have a black spot about 2 inches long and a quarter of an inch wide showing.<P>  have i really screwed up, or can I just hit it with some grey primer enamel, or do I need some special primer to stick to the plastic?||||reg||1
Z||000001||fyerstarter||05-16-2001||12:59 AM||||Well its not actually plastic its "hood material" :P   Regular primer should work, I sanded me entire car down and rattle can primered it over a year ago and its still sticking fine. One of these years I'll get it painted.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||05-16-2001||01:12 AM||||The black you found is the factory primer and you may repaint it with no problem. (the composite material is white)<BR>When wet sanding clear coat run-off will be white. Pay attention to color change as the base coat (color coat) is VERY thin!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||05-16-2001||07:43 AM||||When I got NOS parts for my 88, it came as a molded black plastic. Of course some stone chips on my new repainted front fascia, show through as black as do the stone chips on my 87's front fascia. My old 88 front fascia when sanded down to the base was black.<P>You've sanded down to the bare "metal" as on metal cars. Primer, paint and clear coat.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||05-16-2001||08:17 AM||||Two important things:<BR>If you use primer from a spray can, then use touch-up paint froma spray can. But don't try to apply professional grade paint over spray can primer. They don't work together. The primer will eventually react with the paint.  And if you *do* go to the base material, use a heavy fill primer, and wet sand it well before applying the paint. If you can see any imperfections before painting, you'll seem them after. Paint doesn't fix flaws. <BR>Two: If you go through the paint on the fascia to the base material, use an adhesion promoter like "Bulldog", then prime and paint. <BR>Good luck...<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||09:48 AM||||thanks everyone, I feel better now. The orginal owner said something about hitting a deer once and the repair guy and overspray.<P>(its a squirrel squashing deer smacking driving machine, the Fiero! The Fiero!) -the Canyon-arrow ad from the Simpsons<P>  I think the silver paint I took off just under the peeling clear coat was a poor attempt at repairing the hood. The metal flake under it looks really nice, but there must be some body putty or something on the hood or the headlight panels or somewhere up there, otherwise he wouldnt have painted the whole hood like that. <BR> <BR>  Will definatenly wet sand the whole hood down and get rid of the leporsy look. Still havent decided on a color for the car.<P>  We will have two saturns left when I sell my 2000 SL, the 94 SL2 homecoming special, and the L200 Im getting for my wife (anyday now when the paper work comes through) and they are both white. It would be cool to paint the fiero white, they do look related with the full across back tail lights, and they are related via their GM heritage - so having all three white in the driveway would look cool.<P>  (this reminds me of a story, a small company I worked at, about 8 of us all had white cars, and a few others had cars of different colors. I noticed how neat it looked when all the white cars were parked together in front of the company - small building in a wealthy suburb. So I proposed during coffee break one day we should put up a sign in the front parking lot "White only! Colored park behind the building". The african-american service technician I worked with almost died laughing - his car was brown. We would never have acutally put the sign up, but it would have made a great april 1st joke).<P>My other idea is, since I might use my fiero to tow my Hobie Cat 16 (360 lb beach cat) around, I might paint it the same sky blue as the hobie. I cant think of a classier tow-motor for a hobie cat than a fiero with the sunroof out, and it would look slick if they matched color - or would that be too tacky?<P><p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
